<ul>
	<li>Good decks/cards should be concise, exhaustive, explicit and concrete. Abstractions/generalizations/vague statements are fine, but they should be preceded by the concrete points that "proves" it.</li>
	<li>Start with atomic cards, then make cards that synthesizes those cards in e.g. enumerations, general concepts etc. e.g. 3 cards on defending when in check, on moving out of check, interposing check, capturing attacker. Then do enumeration "Which 3 ways can you defend check" concept "Why would all other moves than one of the 3 ways of defending check lead to loss?" "Because any other move that doesnt move the king out of check, capturing the piece attacking the king or blocking the attack on the king would, if legal, let the opponent capture the king on their next turn"
</ul>
